Node.js compatibility is controlled by the `nodejs_compat` flag in your Wrangler config, so remove it from `compatibility_flags`:

```jsonc
// wrangler.jsonc
{
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",
  "compatibility_flags": []   // no "nodejs_compat"
}
```

Also remove `nodejs_compat_v2` if it's there, then redeploy. Any code or dependency that imports `node:*` modules will then fail to build or run.

I'm not aware of a change that turns Node.js compatibility on by default for dates after August 2026, and I can't verify one. If your Worker still has Node APIs after removing the flag, check the Cloudflare compatibility flags docs for a `no_`-prefixed opt-out flag. Those docs list how each flag behaves at your compatibility date.

Use `mockImplementation` (or pass the implementation to `vi.fn`) and branch on the arguments:

```ts
const getUser = vi.fn((id: number) => {
  if (id === 1) return { name: 'Alice' }
  if (id === 2) return { name: 'Bob' }
  return null
})
```

Or equivalently: `getUser.mockImplementation((id) => ...)`.

For a fixed sequence of return values regardless of arguments, chain `mockReturnValueOnce(a).mockReturnValueOnce(b).mockReturnValue(defaultValue)`.

Vitest has no built-in `when(...)`-style matcher, but the `vitest-when` package adds one: `when(fn).calledWith(1).thenReturn({ name: 'Alice' })`.
